text:
emma: quick q — we keep getting bugs from customers using our JS SDK across different runtimes. node CJS bundles complain about undici being ESM-only, edge runtimes already have global fetch, and some folks on Deno see type mismatches. what's our recommended approach for a single distributable?

raj: short answer: don't force undici on environments that already provide fetch. prefer a tiny runtime-detection shim and conditional exports. :+1:

dan: agree. shipping both esm + cjs builds and an adapter layer is the most compatible. users on Node 14/16 can keep using CJS bundles; node 18+ will usually have global fetch but many apps still rely on undici for consistent behavior.

zoe: also remember types — TS needs to see fetch types (lib.dom). for node consumers we add a small ambient .d.ts or rely on "dom" lib in tsconfig for SDK dev builds. otherwise people get "fetch not found" in their editors. :thinking:

emma: can we post a concrete pattern? a tiny example would help (we can add to the SDK README).

raj: here's the pattern we've started recommending internally: a single runtime adapter module that resolves at runtime and falls back to undici only when global fetch is missing. keep top-level SDK code import-agnostic and use that adapter. example: 

```ts
// src/runtime/fetch.ts
export async function getFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  // dynamic import keeps undici out of edge bundles
  const { fetch } = await import('undici');
  return fetch;
}
```

// usage
```ts
const fetch = await getFetch();
const res = await fetch(url, opts);
```

Note: avoid top-level await in CJS consumers — export an async fn.

dan: for CJS compatibility compile a cjs wrapper that re-exports the async fn as CommonJS. also use package.json conditional exports so bundlers pick the right entry: 

```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
  },
  "./runtime/fetch": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/runtime/fetch.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/runtime/fetch.cjs"
  }
}
```

raj: small caveat: undici v5 is ESM-first. dynamic import works in Node (even CJS it's fine because import() is supported), but if someone pins an older CJS-only undici it's messy. recommend depending on undici@^5 and documenting that Node <16 should polyfill fetch upstream.

zoe: Deno: expose a Deno-specific adapter in our exports map that just returns globalThis.fetch and has proper types. e.g. add a "deno" conditional in README and examples.

emma: what about bundlers (webpack/rollup/parcel)? we don't want undici ending up in browser bundles.

raj: because it's a dynamic import behind a runtime check, most bundlers won't include undici in browser builds. but add package.json "sideEffects": false and explicit exports so bundlers resolve correctly. also call out in docs: if you bundle for node, mark undici as a devDependency or peerDependency? we prefer it as a normal dependency (so server installs get it automatically) but mention the implications.

dan: for tests and local dev we add a tiny node-fetch shim in jest setup to avoid flakiness. ship a test-utils helper `withNodeFetch` that installs a fetch polyfill in globalThis so unit tests don't need network or undici.

zoe: types: two options — rely on lib.dom for consumer TS projects or ship a small types file: 

```ts
// types/fetch.d.ts
export type Fetcher = (input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;
```

and in our public API we type functions to accept `fetch: Fetcher` or default to internal getFetch. keeps signatures explicit and helps consumers substitute mocks.

emma: thumbs up. i'll draft a README snippet with the adapter pattern + the package.json exports example. can we also add a short troubleshooting section: "If you see import undici ESM error -> upgrade node or use the cjs entry".

deploy-bot: deploy check — lint passed for README change branch (ci/1234) :white_check_mark:

raj: one more tiny tip — prefer to call the shim module something like `@redwood/runtime-fetch` internal path so users rarely import it directly and we can iterate. if users do import it directly, we keep the public types stable.

emma: awesome. i'll open a PR with the adapter + docs and tag you all for review. :rocket:
